**ArrayList** stores elements in a contiguous `Object[]` array. Index access is `elementData[index]` — a single memory read. Constant time regardless of list size.

**LinkedList** stores elements in `Node` objects scattered across the heap. To get element at index `i`, Java starts from the head and calls `node.next` repeatedly:

```java
// LinkedList.get(i) simplified:
Node<E> x = first;
for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
    x = x.next;
return x.item;
```

LinkedList does optimize slightly: if `i > size/2`, it traverses backward from the tail. But the worst case is still O(n/2) = **O(n)**.

Practical implication: never call `get(i)` in a loop on a LinkedList — that's O(n²). Use an Iterator instead.
